  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nHot and humid (at least for this region) conditions are forecast\nfor the latter half of the week as the subtropical ridge will\nstrengthen once again with peak H5 heights likely reaching \n597-599dm over northern Arizona, southern Nevada, and southern \nUtah. The strength looks to be just shy of what just occurred this\npast weekend, but guidance shows the ridge center to our north \ninstead of directly over central Arizona. \n\nThe current Extreme Heat Warning goes through Tuesday with the\nHeatRisk decreasing to very localized Major on Wednesday. \nHowever, this decrease in HeatRisk is largely due to the increase \nin moisture as forecast heat indices really do not go down. \nForecast highs Wednesday range from 107-110 degrees in Phoenix to \n108-111 degrees across the western lower deserts, however heat \nindices should stay between 110-115 degrees as dew points surge \ninto the 60s to as high as the lower 70s across far southwest \nArizona and southeast California. Beyond Wednesday, a resurgence \nof the ridge is expected to begin to raise temperatures again with\nhighs drifting a degree or two higher by Friday. Unless things \nchange, it seems likely the Extreme Heat Warning will be extended \nfor a good portion of the area through the rest of the week. \nHowever, any bigger convective day could disrupt the atmosphere \nleading to a slightly cooler day. \n\nMonsoon storm potential for the rest of the week looks to be\nfairly low to at most mid grade with 20-40% chances most days over\nthe eastern Arizona high terrain to 10-20% into the Arizona lower\ndeserts. There are some hints at a potential increase in\nconvective activity maybe by the weekend into early next week, but\nwe do not put much faith in the long range models for monsoon \nactivity. Although, as long as the high center ends up staying \npositioned far enough to our north, it at least seems quite \nplausible any minor easterly wave could march on through \nproviding enhanced chances at some point.",
